// GlobalInitOrder — WHICH ORDER `__module_init` FILLS THE MANAGED GLOBALS IN, and the refusal for a program
// that admits no order at all.
//
// ⚖ **A `static let` / `static var` INITIALIZER RUNS EAGERLY, BEFORE `main`, EXACTLY ONCE** (user ruling),
// and so does a module-level `var`'s: the two are one population (`ModuleInit`). The initializer expression
// itself may not name another global — `ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl` answers `notFound` for a managed
// decl, so `let A = "x"` / `let B = A` is E2004 — but it may CALL, and what a callee's BODY reads is not
// bounded by that rule. `type Outer / static var value = Outer.build()` whose `build()` calls `Inner.get()`
// reads `Inner`'s slot, so `Inner` must be filled first.
//
// ⛔⛔ **DECLARATION ORDER IS NOT DEPENDENCY ORDER, AND USING IT WOULD BE A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** A program
// may declare `Outer` above `Inner`; run in declaration order, `Outer.build()` loads a slot the image left at
// zero and answers with a bogus number or dereferences null. Neither is a diagnostic — which is why the order
// is DERIVED here, from the reads the program actually makes.
//
// ⭐⭐ **TWO EDGE SETS, BECAUSE ORDERING AND REFUSING NEED OPPOSITE ERRORS.** An over-approximate edge set is
// exactly right for choosing an ORDER — a superset of the true edges still yields a valid order whenever it
// is acyclic — and is exactly wrong for REFUSING, where two invented edges are a compile error on a working
// program. So the walk runs twice:
//
//   • `EdgeConfidence.named` — only what an op itself names. A cycle here is a cycle the program has, and it
//     is the ONLY thing that raises E2012.
//   • `EdgeConfidence.possible` — every function the reachability walk can credit. It may only ADD order
//     constraints; an edge of this kind that would close a cycle is DROPPED, never reported.
//
// ⚠ **"NAMED" IS ABOUT WHICH FUNCTION, NOT ABOUT WHETHER THE BRANCH RUNS.** A call under an `if` is an edge
// like any other — that is what every reachability walk in this compiler means by a call graph, and what the
// cycle case in `specs-shv2/lazy-static-observable.md` rests on. What `named` excludes is an edge to a
// function the op does not name at all: a member of an OVERLOAD SET (a call op carries the set's base name
// until `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls`, which runs after this), a minted parameter-default helper,
// and the witness impls a reached conformer's tables might dispatch through.
//
// ⭐ **THE EVIDENCE IS THE EMITTED `globalAddr`, AND IT IS BIDIRECTIONAL** — the same op, read the same way,
// as `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`. A WRITE opens with `globalAddr` exactly as a read does, so an
// initializer that assigns another global's slot depends on it too; that is what keeps
// `Parser.emitCheckedGlobalStore`'s unconditional decref of the old value correct when the store is issued
// from inside an initializer.
//
// ⭐ **THE EDGE PRIMITIVES ARE `StdlibSource`'s** — `markMaxonCalleeEdge` for a direct call / `functionRef` /
// `serviceSend`, `markReachable` for the overload-set and parameter-default widening,
// `markConformerWitnessImpls` for a reached type's witness impls, `markOneReachable` for the unwidened edge.
// It is deliberately NOT the same WALK, and both divergences are in the safe direction for the set that
// reads them:
//
//   • no skip list reaches `markConformerWitnessImpls` (`StdlibSource` passes the six builtin conformers,
//     because a SEED credits those there and nothing seeds this walk), so `possible` credits MORE;
//   • the W17 builtin-conformer seed and the second drain `StdlibSource.reachableMaxonFunctionNames` runs
//     are absent, so `possible` credits LESS than that walk would. Both are order constraints only, and a
//     missing one can cost an order this graph never had a reason to fix.
//
// ⚠ **COST: TWO CALL-GRAPH WALKS PER GLOBAL WHOSE INITIALIZER CALLS, and none at all for a program with no
// such global.** A global whose record the compiler MATERIALIZES (a String literal, an array literal, an
// empty container, a payload-free union case) runs no program code and can depend on nothing, so it is never
// a walk root. ⚠ That is NOT "no program pays": a live `CharacterSet` preset is a calling-initializer global,
// so any program that reads one has roots here.

// A global's position in the list `__module_init` fills — ARENA order on the way in
// (`ProgramSignatures.managedGlobals`) and dependency order on the way out. The cycle report reads
// "later-declared" off it, exactly as the top-level constant evaluator reads it off an arena index.
typealias GlobalInitIndex = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias GlobalInitIndexArray = Array with GlobalInitIndex
typealias GlobalInitIndexSet = Set with GlobalInitIndex
typealias GlobalInitIndexByLabel = Map with (ByteArray, GlobalInitIndex)

// One row per global: the globals its initializer reads, as positions in the incoming list.
typealias GlobalInitDependencies = Array with GlobalInitIndexArray

// A frame's depth on the ordering walk's explicit stack. Its own alias because it indexes the FRAMES and not
// the globals, and the two spaces coincide only while every global is on the stack at once.
typealias GlobalInitStackDepth = int(0 to u64.max)

// How far a frame has got through its global's dependency row — a position in that ROW, which is a third
// space again.
typealias GlobalInitCursor = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias GlobalInitCursorArray = Array with GlobalInitCursor

// How much a walk may credit — see the header's two-edge-sets paragraph.
enum EdgeConfidence
	named
	possible
end 'EdgeConfidence'

// Where the ordering DFS stands on one global.
enum GlobalInitVisit
	unvisited
	onTheStack
	settled
end 'GlobalInitVisit'

typealias GlobalInitVisitArray = Array with GlobalInitVisit

// `markConformerWitnessImpls` skips conformers a SEED owns, because in `StdlibSource`'s walk something else
// credits them. Nothing seeds this walk, so it owns every conformer it meets and the skip list is empty —
// which over-credits, and over-crediting is the safe direction for the only set that reads it.
let noSeededConformers = ByteArrayArray.create()

// ⭐ **THE GLOBALS, REORDERED SO THAT EVERY GLOBAL FOLLOWS THE ONES ITS INITIALIZER READS**, or a refusal.
// Called once per compile, on the merged Maxon module, immediately before `installManagedGlobalInit` — the
// same position `liveGlobalLabels` occupies and for the same reason: `__module_init` opens every global with
// a `globalAddr` of its own slot, so a walk run afterwards would find every global depending on itself.
export function dependencyOrderedGlobals(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, globals ManagedGlobalArray) returns ManagedGlobalArray throws CompileError
	if not anyInitializerCalls(globals) 'nothingRunsProgramCode'
		return globals
	end 'nothingRunsProgramCode'

	// The index can see no duplicate the pipeline's own does not — it is built BEFORE the two synthesized
	// functions exist, exactly as `StdlibSource.reachableMaxonFunctionNames`' is — so refusing here reports
	// the same E4015, at the same declaration, that `PassPipeline.run` would report one screen later.
	let index = try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project)

	let named = initDependencies(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, index: index, globals: globals, edges: EdgeConfidence.named)
	let possible = initDependencies(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, index: index, globals: globals, edges: EdgeConfidence.possible)

	return orderedByDependency(globals, deps: try buildEffectiveDeps(globals, named: named, possible: possible, project: project))
end 'dependencyOrderedGlobals'

// Does any global's initializer run program code at all? The walk's whole apparatus — a function-name index,
// two per-root reachability scans — is skipped when none does, which is the answer for a program whose
// globals are all literals.
function anyInitializerCalls(globals ManagedGlobalArray) returns bool
	for global in globals 'eachGlobal'
		if global.initCallees.count() > 0 'runsProgramCode'
			return true
		end 'runsProgramCode'
	end 'eachGlobal'

	return false
end 'anyInitializerCalls'

// ⭐ **THE GRAPH THE ORDER IS TAKEN FROM, BUILT EDGE BY EDGE SO A NAMED CYCLE IS THE ONLY THING THAT CAN
// REFUSE.** Every NAMED edge goes in, and one that closes a cycle is E2012 — both halves are edges the
// program's own ops name, so the cycle is real. Every remaining POSSIBLE edge then goes in unless it closes a
// cycle, in which case it is dropped: it may name no call the program makes, and refusing a working program
// is the failure this split exists to prevent.
//
// ⚠ **A DROPPED EDGE IS A RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN.** Were such an edge real, the order it would
// have forced is lost and that global reads a zero. The alternative is refusing every program whose overload
// members happen to cross-reference — MEASURED, and the defect this split repairs. Removing the residual
// means resolving overloads, which happens after this point in the pipeline.
function buildEffectiveDeps(globals ManagedGlobalArray, named GlobalInitDependencies, possible GlobalInitDependencies, project Project) returns GlobalInitDependencies throws CompileError
	var effective = GlobalInitDependencies.create()
	for _ in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachGlobal'
		effective.push(GlobalInitIndexArray.create())
	end 'eachGlobal'

	for i in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachNamedSource'
		for dependency in rowOf(named, index: i) 'eachNamedEdge'
			try addDependencyEdge(effective, from: i, to: dependency, edges: EdgeConfidence.named, globals: globals, project: project)
		end 'eachNamedEdge'
	end 'eachNamedSource'

	for j in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachPossibleSource'
		for dependency in rowOf(possible, index: j) 'eachPossibleEdge'
			try addDependencyEdge(effective, from: j, to: dependency, edges: EdgeConfidence.possible, globals: globals, project: project)
		end 'eachPossibleEdge'
	end 'eachPossibleSource'

	return effective
end 'buildEffectiveDeps'

// ⭐ **ONE EDGE, AND THE CONFIDENCE DECIDES ONLY WHAT A CYCLE MEANS.** `to` already reaching `from` makes the
// two mutually dependent: by roads the program's own ops name, that is E2012; by a road the walk merely
// credited, it is an edge to drop.
function addDependencyEdge(effective GlobalInitDependencies, from GlobalInitIndex, to GlobalInitIndex, edges EdgeConfidence, globals ManagedGlobalArray, project Project) throws CompileError
	if rowContains(effective, index: from, value: to) 'alreadyAnEdge'
		return
	end 'alreadyAnEdge'

	if reachesBack(effective, from: from, to: to) 'mightCloseACycle'
		let arc = pathBetween(effective, from: to, to: from)
		if arc.count() > 0 'closesACycle'
			match edges 'edges'
				named then try refuseInitializationCycle(arc, globals: globals, project: project)
				possible then return
			end 'edges'
		end 'closesACycle'
	end 'mightCloseACycle'

	var row = rowOf(effective, index: from)
	row.push(to)
end 'addDependencyEdge'

// ⚠ **THE CHEAP HALF OF THE CYCLE TEST, AND IT IS A COST BOUND RATHER THAN AN ANSWER.** A node whose row is
// still empty reaches nothing but itself, so only a SELF edge can close a cycle through it. Without this the
// exact test below walks everything reachable from `to` once per edge, which is quadratic in a program whose
// globals form one long chain — the shape a 10,000-deep probe is made of.
function reachesBack(effective GlobalInitDependencies, from GlobalInitIndex, to GlobalInitIndex) returns bool
	return to == from or rowOf(effective, index: to).count() > 0
end 'reachesBack'

// ⭐ **A PATH FROM `from` TO `to`, OR AN EMPTY ARRAY.** It is asked only when the caller is about to add the
// closing edge `to -> from`, so the path IS the cycle's arc — and `from == to` answers with the
// single-element path that a self-reading initializer is.
//
// Iterative, with a parent column, for `orderedByDependency`'s reason: nothing here may recurse to the depth
// of a user's dependency chain.
function pathBetween(deps GlobalInitDependencies, from GlobalInitIndex, to GlobalInitIndex) returns GlobalInitIndexArray
	var seen = BoolArray.create()
	seen.resize(deps.count())
	var parent = GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
	for _ in 0 upto deps.count() 'eachGlobal'
		parent.push(0)
	end 'eachGlobal'

	var stack = GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
	stack.push(from)
	try seen.set(from, value: true) otherwise panic("pathBetween: seen.set OOB at {from}, outside the graph its own rows describe")

	while stack.count() > 0 'walk'
		let node = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("pathBetween: pop from a non-empty stack")
		if node == to 'arrived'
			return pathBackFrom(node, from: from, parent: parent)
		end 'arrived'

		for next in rowOf(deps, index: node) 'eachEdge'
			let queued = try seen.get(next) otherwise panic("pathBetween: seen.get OOB at {next}, outside the graph its own rows describe")
			if queued 'alreadyQueued'
				continue
			end 'alreadyQueued'

			try seen.set(next, value: true) otherwise panic("pathBetween: seen.set OOB at {next}, outside the graph its own rows describe")
			try parent.set(next, value: node) otherwise panic("pathBetween: parent.set OOB at {next}, outside the graph its own rows describe")
			stack.push(next)
		end 'eachEdge'
	end 'walk'

	return GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
end 'pathBetween'

// The walked path, source first. `from`'s own parent entry is never read, which is what lets the climb
// terminate on it rather than on a sentinel.
function pathBackFrom(node GlobalInitIndex, from GlobalInitIndex, parent GlobalInitIndexArray) returns GlobalInitIndexArray
	var reversed = GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
	var walker = node
	reversed.push(walker)
	while walker != from 'climb'
		walker = try parent.get(walker) otherwise panic("pathBackFrom: parent.get OOB at {walker}, outside the column the search filled")
		reversed.push(walker)
	end 'climb'

	var path = GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto reversed.count() 'eachStep'
		path.push(try reversed.get(reversed.count() - 1 - i) otherwise panic("pathBackFrom: reversed.get OOB inside a loop bounded by its own count"))
	end 'eachStep'

	return path
end 'pathBackFrom'

// One dependency row. The bound is the caller's own loop or a position this walk minted, so a miss is an
// invariant break rather than a case.
function rowOf(deps GlobalInitDependencies, index GlobalInitIndex) returns GlobalInitIndexArray
	return try deps.get(index) otherwise panic("rowOf: deps.get OOB at {index} — one row is built per global")
end 'rowOf'

function rowContains(deps GlobalInitDependencies, index GlobalInitIndex, value GlobalInitIndex) returns bool
	for member in rowOf(deps, index: index) 'eachMember'
		if member == value 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachMember'

	return false
end 'rowContains'

// One dependency row per global, in the incoming order.
//
// The `reached` array is allocated ONCE and cleared between roots rather than reallocated: the walks are
// independent, but the array is a function of the module and not of the root.
function initDependencies(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, index FunctionNameIndex, globals ManagedGlobalArray, edges EdgeConfidence) returns GlobalInitDependencies
	var slotOf = GlobalInitIndexByLabel.create()
	for i in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachGlobal'
		slotOf.upsert(globalAt(globals, index: i).label, value: i)
	end 'eachGlobal'

	var reached = BoolArray.create()
	reached.resize(module.funcCount())
	var names = FunctionNameSet.create()

	var deps = GlobalInitDependencies.create()
	for j in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachRoot'
		let global = globalAt(globals, index: j)
		if global.initCallees.count() == 0 'dependsOnNothing'
			deps.push(GlobalInitIndexArray.create())
			continue
		end 'dependsOnNothing'

		clearReached(reached)
		deps.push(slotsReadFrom(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, index: index, roots: global.initCallees, slotOf: slotOf, reached: reached, names: names, edges: edges))
	end 'eachRoot'

	return deps
end 'initDependencies'

function clearReached(reached BoolArray)
	for i in 0 upto reached.count() 'eachFunction'
		try reached.set(i, value: false) otherwise panic("clearReached: reached.set OOB at {i}, inside a loop bounded by its own count")
	end 'eachFunction'
end 'clearReached'

// Every global slot named by any function reachable from `roots` at this confidence — the initializer's own
// call tree, walked to a fixpoint.
function slotsReadFrom(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, index FunctionNameIndex, roots ByteArrayArray, slotOf GlobalInitIndexByLabel, reached BoolArray, names FunctionNameSet, edges EdgeConfidence) returns GlobalInitIndexArray
	var found = GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
	var claimed = GlobalInitIndexSet.create()
	var worklist = FuncIndexArray.create()

	for root in roots 'eachRoot'
		markCalleeEdge(root, project: project, edges: edges, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachRoot'

	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("slotsReadFrom: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		let func = module.getFunc(idx)
		markWitnessEdges(func, project: project, signatures: signatures, edges: edges, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

		// `block.opRefs` directly, for `StdlibSource.markMaxonCallees`' reason: this runs at the Maxon tier,
		// where a dropped op keeps its `module.ops` index and belongs to no block, so a flat scan of
		// `module.ops` is not the same walk.
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("slotsReadFrom: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("slotsReadFrom: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				markOpCallee(op, project: project, edges: edges, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
				noteSlotNamed(op, slotOf: slotOf, claimed: claimed, found: found)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'walk'

	return found
end 'slotsReadFrom'

// The callee an op binds, credited at this confidence. `possible` is `StdlibSource`'s own edge; `named` asks
// the same dialect classifier and then declines every name that denotes a SET rather than a function.
function markOpCallee(op MaxonOp, project Project, edges EdgeConfidence, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match edges 'edges'
		possible then markMaxonCalleeEdge(op, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		named then markNamedOpCallee(op, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'edges'
end 'markOpCallee'

function markNamedOpCallee(op MaxonOp, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct to functionValue then markCalleeEdge(maxonOpCalleeName(op), project: project, edges: EdgeConfidence.named, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		noCallee then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'
end 'markNamedOpCallee'

// One callee NAME, credited at this confidence.
//
// ⛔ **A NAME AN OVERLOAD SET CLAIMS IS NOT AN EDGE AT `named` CONFIDENCE, AND NEITHER IS ITS FIRST MEMBER.**
// A call op carries the SET's base name until `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` rebinds it, so the
// function that happens to hold the bare spelling is no more the callee than any other member; crediting it
// would be an unprovable edge wearing an exact one's clothes. The same holds for a minted parameter-default
// helper, which the same pass re-points.
function markCalleeEdge(name ByteArray, project Project, edges EdgeConfidence, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match edges 'edges'
		possible then markReachable(name, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		named then markNamedCallee(name, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'edges'
end 'markCalleeEdge'

function markNamedCallee(name ByteArray, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	if project.overloadSets.contains(name) or isParamDefaultHelperName(name) 'namesASetRatherThanAFunction'
		return
	end 'namesASetRatherThanAFunction'

	markOneReachable(name, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
end 'markNamedCallee'

// A reached type's witness impls. A dispatch names a SLOT rather than a body, so which impl runs is a fact
// about the receiver — credited at `possible` and never at `named`.
function markWitnessEdges(func IrFunction, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, edges EdgeConfidence, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match edges 'edges'
		named then break 'edges'
		possible then markConformerWitnessImpls(func, project: project, signatures: signatures, seedConformers: noSeededConformers, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'edges'
end 'markWitnessEdges'

// Record the global an op names, once. A slot named twice in one cone is one dependency, and the set is what
// keeps a row proportional to the globals rather than to the ops that touch them.
function noteSlotNamed(op MaxonOp, slotOf GlobalInitIndexByLabel, claimed GlobalInitIndexSet, found GlobalInitIndexArray)
	if not maxonOpNamesAGlobalLabel(op) 'namesNoSlot'
		return
	end 'namesNoSlot'

	// A label the map does not hold is a SCALAR global — its value is the `.data` image, written before
	// anything runs — or one dead-global elimination dropped. Neither is initialized by this function, so
	// neither can be ordered against.
	let slot = try slotOf.get(maxonOpGlobalLabelName(op)) otherwise 'notAManagedSlot'
		return
	end 'notAManagedSlot'

	if claimed.contains(slot) 'alreadyCredited'
		return
	end 'alreadyCredited'

	claimed.insert(slot)
	found.push(slot)
end 'noteSlotNamed'

// ⭐ **THE TOPOLOGICAL ORDER — a post-order DFS with an EXPLICIT STACK, driven over the incoming list so the
// emitted code is a function of the program.** Ties are broken by declaration order for that reason and no
// other: two globals that depend on nothing may run in either order, and the one that keeps a golden
// fragment stable is the order the arena already fixed.
//
// ⛔ **IT MAY NOT RECURSE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED BOUND RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.** The depth is the user's
// dependency CHAIN, which nothing bounds: a program of 3,000 statics each reading the next overflowed the
// compiler's own stack with no diagnostic and no binary (`STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW`), while the same program
// with the chain broken compiled. The frames are heap arrays here, so depth costs memory and nothing else.
function orderedByDependency(globals ManagedGlobalArray, deps GlobalInitDependencies) returns ManagedGlobalArray
	var state = GlobalInitVisitArray.create()
	for _ in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachGlobal'
		state.push(GlobalInitVisit.unvisited)
	end 'eachGlobal'

	var ordered = ManagedGlobalArray.create()
	var frames = GlobalInitIndexArray.create()
	var cursors = GlobalInitCursorArray.create()

	for root in 0 upto globals.count() 'eachRoot'
		match visitOf(state, index: root) 'root'
			settled then break 'root'
			onTheStack then panic("orderedByDependency: global {root} is on the stack between two roots — every frame this walk pushes it also pops")
			unvisited then descendTo(root, state: state, frames: frames, cursors: cursors)
		end 'root'

		while frames.count() > 0 'walk'
			let depth = frames.count() - 1
			let node = frameAt(frames, depth: depth)
			let cursor = cursorAt(cursors, depth: depth)
			let row = rowOf(deps, index: node)

			if cursor < row.count() 'moreDependencies'
				try cursors.set(depth, value: cursor + 1) otherwise panic("orderedByDependency: cursors.set OOB at depth {depth}, inside the frame this line just read")
				let dependency = try row.get(cursor) otherwise panic("orderedByDependency: row.get OOB at {cursor}, below the count this branch tested")
				match visitOf(state, index: dependency) 'dependency'
					settled then break 'dependency'
					onTheStack then panic("orderedByDependency: global {dependency} closes a cycle — `buildEffectiveDeps` adds no edge that does, so the graph handed here is acyclic by construction")
					unvisited then descendTo(dependency, state: state, frames: frames, cursors: cursors)
				end 'dependency'
				continue
			end 'moreDependencies'

			_ = try frames.pop() otherwise panic("orderedByDependency: pop from the frame stack this loop just read")
			_ = try cursors.pop() otherwise panic("orderedByDependency: pop from the cursor stack this loop just read")
			markVisit(state, index: node, visit: GlobalInitVisit.settled)
			ordered.push(globalAt(globals, index: node))
		end 'walk'
	end 'eachRoot'

	return ordered
end 'orderedByDependency'

// Open a frame on `index` and mark it in progress, in one act — the pair that must not come apart, since the
// stack is what `settled` is decided against.
function descendTo(index GlobalInitIndex, state GlobalInitVisitArray, frames GlobalInitIndexArray, cursors GlobalInitCursorArray)
	markVisit(state, index: index, visit: GlobalInitVisit.onTheStack)
	frames.push(index)
	cursors.push(0)
end 'descendTo'

function visitOf(state GlobalInitVisitArray, index GlobalInitIndex) returns GlobalInitVisit
	return try state.get(index) otherwise panic("visitOf: state.get OOB at {index}, outside the column built over the global list")
end 'visitOf'

function markVisit(state GlobalInitVisitArray, index GlobalInitIndex, visit GlobalInitVisit)
	try state.set(index, value: visit) otherwise panic("markVisit: state.set OOB at {index}, outside the column built over the global list")
end 'markVisit'

function frameAt(frames GlobalInitIndexArray, depth GlobalInitStackDepth) returns GlobalInitIndex
	return try frames.get(depth) otherwise panic("frameAt: frames.get OOB at depth {depth}, above the stack this walk pushed")
end 'frameAt'

function cursorAt(cursors GlobalInitCursorArray, depth GlobalInitStackDepth) returns GlobalInitCursor
	return try cursors.get(depth) otherwise panic("cursorAt: cursors.get OOB at depth {depth}, above the stack this walk pushed")
end 'cursorAt'

// **THE CYCLE.** `arc` is a path whose last member's initializer reads its first, so no member can be filled
// before the others.
//
// It names the ARC and anchors on the LAST-DECLARED member's NAME token — the same two choices
// `ProgramSignatures.recordCycle` makes for a cycle among top-level constants, under the same code, because
// it is the same fact one scope over: `docs/error-codes.txt` states E2012 as the cycle rather than the
// construct it was found in. Naming the whole in-progress SET instead would call a global that merely LEADS
// to a cycle circular, which is what the bootstrap does and what shv2 declined to copy.
//
// ⭐ A ONE-MEMBER ARC IS A DIFFERENT SENTENCE, because a list of one is not an arc an author can follow: it
// says what the single global does rather than leaving them to infer it.
function refuseInitializationCycle(arc GlobalInitIndexArray, globals ManagedGlobalArray, project Project) throws CompileError
	var names = ""
	var owner = try arc.get(0) otherwise panic("refuseInitializationCycle: the arc is empty — its caller adds the closing edge only after proving the path non-empty")
	for i in 0 upto arc.count() 'eachMember'
		let member = try arc.get(i) otherwise panic("refuseInitializationCycle: arc.get OOB at {i}, inside a loop bounded by its own count")
		if i > 0 'separator'
			names = "{names}, "
		end 'separator'

		names = "{names}{String.from(globalAt(globals, index: member).declName)}"
		if member > owner 'laterDeclaration'
			owner = member
		end 'laterDeclaration'
	end 'eachMember'

	let anchor = globalAt(globals, index: owner)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.parserCircularDependency, message: cycleMessage(names, memberCount: arc.count()), line: anchor.nameLine, column: anchor.nameColumn, filePath: anchor.declFilePath)
	throw CompileError.compileError
end 'refuseInitializationCycle'

function cycleMessage(names String, memberCount GlobalInitIndex) returns String
	if memberCount == 1 'readsItsOwnSlot'
		return "Circular dependency detected among global initializers: {names} reads its own slot, directly or through a function its initializer calls"
	end 'readsItsOwnSlot'

	return "Circular dependency detected among global initializers: {names}"
end 'cycleMessage'

// One global by position. The bound is the caller's own loop or a position this walk minted, so a miss is an
// invariant break rather than a case.
function globalAt(globals ManagedGlobalArray, index GlobalInitIndex) returns ManagedGlobal
	return try globals.get(index) otherwise panic("globalAt: globals.get OOB at {index}, outside the list every position in this walk was minted from")
end 'globalAt'
